---
import Presentation from "../../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/foce/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Group Project 1: Terminal Applications</h1>
        <p class="text-sn-theme">End-of-Quarter Summative &mdash; approx. 2&ndash;2.5 weeks</p>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Group Project 1</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">Terminal Applications</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Everything Up to Now, Combined</h2>
              <p>
                Variables, conditionals, loops, and lists &mdash; on your
                own, in a group, building something real.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Two Tracks &mdash; Pick One</h2>
              <ul>
                <li class="fragment"><b class="text-sn-theme">Terminal Game</b> &mdash; turn-based, puzzle, choose-your-own-adventure, or your own idea</li>
                <li class="fragment"><b class="text-sn-theme">Utility Application</b> &mdash; something that actually helps someone do a real task</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>"Success Criteria" &mdash; What Does That Even Mean?</h2>
              <p>
                Not "my game is fun." A success criterion is something you
                could actually check yes or no on when the project's done.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                <i>"The player can lose all their lives and see a Game Over message."</i>
                That's checkable. "It's fun" isn't.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>The Complexity Bar</h2>
              <p>
                Your project has to genuinely use a mix of what this unit
                taught &mdash; conditionals, a loop, and real list usage.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                A reskinned Dungeon Crawler with no loops and no lists
                doesn't meet that bar, no matter how good the story is.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>The MYP Design Cycle, One More Time</h2>
              <table class="table table-dark table-sm">
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>Criterion</th><th>In This Project</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr><td>A: Inquiring &amp; Analysing</td><td>Idea, similar products, success criteria</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>B: Developing Ideas</td><td>Feature plan, menu flowchart</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>C: Creating the Solution</td><td>The working program</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>D: Evaluating</td><td>Test plan, playtester feedback, individual reflection</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Keep a Task Journal</h2>
              <p>
                A short entry every work session: what you did, who did
                it, what's next. Filled in as you go &mdash; not
                reconstructed from memory the night before it's due.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Today's Objectives</h2>
              <ul>
                <li>Choose a track and a real project idea</li>
                <li>Write success criteria you could actually check</li>
                <li>Start your task journal</li>
              </ul>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Choose Your Track -->
        <section id="tracks">
          <h2>Choose Your Track</h2>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Option A: Terminal Game</h3>
          <p>
            Any terminal-friendly game: turn-based, puzzle, choose-your-own-adventure,
            or something entirely your own. It needs a clear win/lose
            condition, real player choices that matter, and enough state
            tracking (score, health, inventory, branching paths &mdash;
            your call) that playing it twice doesn't feel identical.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Option B: Utility Application</h3>
          <p>
            A program that solves a real, specific task for a real kind of
            user. It needs to do more than one simple calculation &mdash;
            think a menu system with a few related features, and data
            that builds up over the course of a run (a list of entries
            the program collects, not just one-off math).
          </p>
          <p>
            <b>A good idea starts specific, not a topic.</b> "A sports app"
            isn't a project &mdash; "a program that tracks a team's
            win/loss record across a season and calculates their winning
            percentage" is. Pick one real thing to track, calculate, or
            organize, whatever the subject.
          </p>
          <p>Some starting points (you don't have to use these):</p>
          <ul>
            <li>A school-subject helper (grade calculator, flashcard quiz app)</li>
            <li>A specific sports stat tracker (season win/loss record, points-per-game average)</li>
            <li>A budget or allowance tracker</li>
            <li>A workout or habit log</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Success Criteria -->
        <section id="success-criteria">
          <h2>What "Success Criteria" Means</h2>

          <p>
            A <b class="text-sn-theme">success criterion</b> is a specific,
            checkable thing your finished program must do. Not a feeling
            ("it should be fun" or "it should work well") &mdash; something
            you could point at when the project's done and say yes or no.
          </p>

          <p><b>Not checkable:</b> "The game should be fun and challenging."</p>
          <p><b>Checkable:</b> "The player can lose all their lives and see a Game Over message with their final score."</p>

          <p>
            Write 4&ndash;6 of these for your project before you start
            building. They're what Criterion D evaluates you against at
            the end.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Complexity Bar -->
        <section id="complexity">
          <h2>Meeting the Complexity Bar</h2>

          <p>
            Your program needs to genuinely combine what this unit taught
            &mdash; not just show off one skill in isolation. At minimum:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Conditionals (<code>if</code>/<code>elif</code>/<code>else</code>), including nested ones if your concept calls for it</li>
            <li>At least one loop, chosen for the right reason &mdash; <code>while</code> for something that repeats until a condition changes, <code>for</code> for counting or traversing a list</li>
            <li>Real list usage &mdash; traversal, the accumulator pattern, membership checking, or parallel lists. One hardcoded list that never gets processed doesn't count.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            <b>The trap to avoid:</b> a project that's really just the
            Dungeon Crawler with a new story pasted on top &mdash; nested
            conditionals only, no loops, no lists. It's not that the
            Dungeon Crawler was bad; it's that this project needs to show
            you can do more than that one activity did.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- MYP Criteria -->
        <section id="criteria">
          <h2>The MYP Criteria, In This Project</h2>

          <ul>
            <li><b>A &mdash; Inquiring and Analysing:</b> your idea, who it's for, 1&ndash;2 similar games/apps you looked at, and your success criteria.</li>
            <li><b>B &mdash; Developing Ideas:</b> a feature plan (what/why/how for each feature &mdash; not strict pseudocode) and a menu flowchart.</li>
            <li><b>C &mdash; Creating the Solution:</b> the actual working program, meeting the complexity bar above.</li>
            <li><b>D &mdash; Evaluating:</b> a test plan, feedback from someone outside your group who tried it, and an individual reflection from each student.</li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            Full grading detail is in the rubric your instructor provides,
            along with a student checklist you should keep with you the
            whole project.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Task Journal -->
        <section id="journal">
          <h2>Task Journal</h2>

          <p>
            Every work session (or every couple of days if you work
            outside class), log a short entry: what you worked on, who did
            what, any blockers, and what's next. Your instructor will give
            you a template. Fill it in <b>as you go</b> &mdash; a journal
            reconstructed from memory the night before it's due doesn't
            show your real process, and it'll be obvious.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Timeline -->
        <section id="timeline">
          <h2>Timeline</h2>

          <p>
            Roughly 2&ndash;2.5 weeks, depending on how much time is left
            in the quarter. A rough shape to aim for:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><b>Early:</b> pick your track and idea, research, write success criteria, plan your features and flowchart</li>
            <li><b>Middle:</b> build &mdash; this is where most of your time goes</li>
            <li><b>End:</b> test against your success criteria, get outside feedback, write your individual reflections</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- What You'll Turn In -->
        <section id="submission">
          <h2>What You'll Turn In</h2>

          <ul>
            <li>Your problem statement, similar-products notes, and success criteria</li>
            <li>Your feature plan and menu flowchart</li>
            <li>Your finished, working program</li>
            <li>Your test plan and outside playtester feedback</li>
            <li>Your task journal</li>
            <li>An individual reflection from each group member</li>
          </ul>

          <p class="mt-5 text-center">
            Meet with your group, pick a track, and start with your
            problem statement.
          </p>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
